[; Evaluate an RPN expression by folding over the token sequence, carrying the
   stack as a DOCUMENT ([stack [n …] [n …]]) rather than an opaque list — so the
   intermediate state is inspectable data at every step.
   3 4 + 5 * = 35.
   Rewritten for the v0.8.0 surface (RULED: VC-28); the original was pre-reshape
   (`:using`/`:init`/`:case`/`:yield` colon clauses, `[?fn ($a, $b)]` comma
   params, `nth(x, 1)` call-parens).

   The stack is read with the DESCENDANT axis (`$acc//n`), not the child axis
   (`$acc/n`), and that is forced rather than stylistic: placing a node-set in
   element content nests it as a sequence child — `[stack [n 4] ([n 3], [n 9])]`
   — and the child axis does not see through that grouping, so `$acc/n` reports
   one item after two pushes. Measured 2026-08-25; it is #961, in Cluster A,
   which this session does not touch (VC-27). Switch back to `/n` when #961 is
   settled.

   Also limited by the surface, not by taste: an operator arm rebuilds the stack
   as a single `[n …]`, discarding anything deeper, because there is no sequence
   append (`$concat` is strings only). Correct for this expression; a general RPN
   evaluator wants a real push/pop. ]
[?reduce (3, 4, :plus, 5, :times)
  [using [?fn ($acc $tok)
    [?match $tok
      [case :plus [stack [n [+ [$nth $acc//n 1] [$nth $acc//n 2]]]]]
      [case :times [stack [n [* [$nth $acc//n 1] [$nth $acc//n 2]]]]]
      [else [stack [n $tok] $acc//n]]]]]
  [init [stack]]]
